          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"flaubert-madame-bovary-75e4f690ef36a899\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Rodolphe directly presents his love for Emma as a fact: “je vous aime, voilà tout.” Although the avowal is part of a calculated seduction and may be insincere, deception does not alter its strongly truth-conditional mode. It contains no substantial commitment or relational undertaking, and the controlled rhetorical performance supplies no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The declaration advances Rodolphe’s courtship, but that strategic interpersonal function does not by itself establish performative commitment. His actual sincerity remains doubtful but is orthogonal to the classification.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Rodolphe directly tells Emma, “Non, je vous aime, voilà tout!” during a private attempt to seduce her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The surrounding narration frames the declaration as calculated seduction. Rodolphe deliberately stayed away to intensify Emma’s attachment, recognized that his “calcul” had succeeded, and then delivered elaborate romantic rhetoric before making the avowal and soliciting confirmation from her. Emma is strongly affected because such language is new to her. This manipulative framing undermines confidence in his sincerity, but the core utterance still presents love as true rather than undertaking a commitment or operating as a reflexive affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Non, je vous aime, voilà tout! Vous n’en doutez pas!”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense avowal presents Rodolphe’s loving state as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Before returning, Rodolphe reasons that delaying will make Emma love him more; the narrator says “il comprit que son calcul avait été bon.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration occurs within a deliberate seduction strategy, making sincerity doubtful while also weighing against a reflex-like E reading.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rodolphe delivers an extended sequence of rehearsed-sounding romantic claims and “insensiblement” slides from the stool to the floor.\",\"supports\":\"His controlled theatrical conduct supports interpreting the declaration as strategic rhetoric rather than an involuntary expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After the avowal he asks Emma to confirm that she does not doubt it: “Dites-le-moi; un mot! un seul mot!”\",\"supports\":\"The request treats the love claim as something to be believed and acknowledged; it does not add a clear undertaking or obligation.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative present-tense French “je vous aime” in Gustave Flaubert’s Madame Bovary.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified core form and occurrence location.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the declaration’s core force. Its calculated, potentially deceptive use is contextual and does not require O; neither P nor E has independent support.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
